19:53:10 [69d7] --- CONCEPT GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
19:53:10 [69d7] Concept level (ages 8): School-age conceptual reasoning involving explicit category relations, cross-classification, hierarchical reasoning, causal explanation, food-origin/processing reasoning, problem-solution reasoning, and structured narrative understanding.
19:53:10 [69d7] Wording: Questions can be longer and can ask for explanation, comparison, or reasoning, but should remain concrete and contextually grounded. Phrase every question as ONE simple spoken clause a child would hear in conversation; split complex ideas into the question frames below instead of packing them into one sentence.
19:53:10 [69d7] Question length: 8-14 words when expressive language level supports it.
19:53:10 [69d7] Avoid: fully abstract reasoning without concrete context; adult-level nutrition or biology mechanisms; technical food production; highly ambiguous category exceptions; complex social inference without story support; academic connector phrases such as 'in terms of', 'with respect to', 'in what way'; two asks packed into one question (e.g. 'How are X and Y alike in terms of what they are made from?' — use 'What are X and Y both made from?' instead)
19:53:10 [69d7] Use these simple question frames (one clause each): How are X and Y alike? | Why does X belong to this category? | Can X belong to two categories? | What is X made from? | Why did the character do that? | What happened after X? | What should the character do next?
19:53:10 [69d7] Target these concepts (vary across the question set):
19:53:10 [69d7] - within food category: Ask whether a food belongs to a specific food category. e.g. Is an apple a fruit?
19:53:10 [69d7] - typicality reasoning: Use typical examples for simple quizzes; use less typical examples only for advanced reasoning or discussion. e.g. Is an apple a fruit?
19:53:10 [69d7] - cross classification: Ask whether one item can be classified in multiple valid ways. e.g. Is an apple both fruit and food?
19:53:10 [69d7] - superordinate subordinate reasoning: Ask whether an item is a kind of another category, or whether one category is broader than another. e.g. Is a poodle a kind of dog?
19:53:10 [69d7] - origin and processing reasoning: Ask about natural source, animal source, ingredient source, or simple processing pathway. e.g. Do apples grow on trees?
19:53:10 [69d7] - causal explanation: Ask why something happens or why an object/action is useful. e.g. Why do we use umbrellas?
19:53:10 [69d7] - familiar event script: Ask about common actions, objects, people, or places in familiar events. e.g. Do books go in backpacks?
19:53:10 [69d7] - event sequence and planning: Ask whether an action happens before, after, or to prepare for a familiar event. e.g. Do we pack before school?
